        "title": "White-Collar Crime: Sutherland and Beyond",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "**Organizational-dynamics bridge:** Diane Vaughan's \"normalization of deviance\" — the process by which warnings are incrementally rationalized as acceptable until catastrophic failure — was developed from the Challenger disaster but applies directly to corporate crime. The organizational mechanism is identical to technical debt accumulation: each small violation that goes unpunished resets the threshold of what counts as normal; compliance costs are deferred; warnings are reframed as acceptable risk; eventually the system fails catastrophically (Enron, Boeing 737 MAX, Volkswagen emissions). In engineering orgs, the structural conditions that produce this — performance pressure from above, diffuse responsibility (\"someone else will catch it\"), and the absence of independent auditors with real power — are the same conditions Vaughan identifies in corporate crime. The Fraud Triangle (pressure + opportunity + rationalization) maps precisely to: unreachable quarterly targets (pressure), access to complex financial instruments without effective controls (opportunity), and \"everyone does it\" / \"the lawyers signed off\" (rationalization)."
    },
